faqs:
  - question: "What is a project environment in Appwrite?"
    answer: "A project environment is a separate backend that belongs to an Appwrite Cloud project, such as `staging` or `dev`. It has its own databases, storage, users, functions, sites, and settings, and it shares the project's endpoint, project ID, organization, region, and bill. The project itself is production. See [Project environments](/docs/advanced/environments)."
  - question: "How does my app connect to an Appwrite environment?"
    answer: "With the same endpoint and project ID as production, plus the environment's ID. SDKs take it with `setEnvironment`, the REST API with the `X-Appwrite-Environment` header, the CLI with `environmentId` in `appwrite.config.json` or the `APPWRITE_ENVIRONMENT_ID` variable, and the MCP server with `environment_id`. See [Connect to an environment](/docs/advanced/environments/connect)."
  - question: "What does an environment copy from my project?"
    answer: "When you create an environment as a clone of the project, Appwrite copies the structure and settings of databases, storage, auth, functions, sites, messaging, and project settings, and data such as rows, files, and users only when you tick it. API keys, webhooks, custom domains, message history, and backups are never copied. See [Copy a project into an environment](/docs/advanced/environments/copy)."
  - question: "How many environments can an Appwrite project have?"
    answer: "Five per project on the Free and Pro plans, and 25 per project on Enterprise by default. Production, the project itself, isn't counted. See [Limits and billing](/docs/advanced/environments/limits)."
  - question: "Do Appwrite environments cost extra?"
    answer: "No. An environment doesn't count as a project on your plan and isn't charged as an additional project. Its usage is added to the project's usage, and the plan's included resources cover the project and its environments together."
  - question: "Can I run production traffic in an environment?"
    answer: "Environments are built for development and testing, so each one is limited to 100 users and 500 API requests per hour. Requests from the Appwrite Console don't count toward that limit. Production traffic belongs on the project itself."
  - question: "Are project environments available on self-hosted Appwrite?"
    answer: "No. Project environments are an Appwrite Cloud feature."
---

Appwrite Cloud projects now have **environments**. A project can carry development, staging, and QA backends next to production, each with its own databases, storage, users, functions, sites, and settings, all under the same project ID and on the same bill.

An environment starts empty or as a copy of the project, with the structure copied and data only where it's wanted. Apps switch between production and an environment with one extra value, so the same build can run against either. It has been one of the most requested features on Appwrite, and it's live today on the Free, Pro, and Enterprise plans.

# Why teams asked for environments

Most teams on Appwrite already work this way. They keep a second project for staging and sometimes a third for development, and they copy schema changes between them by hand or with the CLI. It works, but every copy of the backend is a separate project with its own ID, its own settings to keep in sync, and its own place on the bill. Promoting a change means remembering which project is which, and a staging project that drifts from production stops being a useful test.

Environments keep all of that under one project. Production stays where it is, and each environment sits beside it as an isolated backend that knows which project it belongs to.

# What an environment is

An environment is a complete Appwrite backend that belongs to a project:

- **Its own resources.** Databases, tables, rows, buckets, files, users, teams, functions, sites, messaging, and project settings are separate from production's. A row written in `staging` never shows up in production, and a test account created in `dev` exists only in `dev`.
- **The project's identity.** An environment has no project ID of its own. It shares the project's endpoint, project ID, organization, and region, and it's addressed by an environment ID such as `staging`.
- **The project's bill.** An environment doesn't count as a project. Its usage is added to the project's.

Inside the Console, the environment switcher in the project sidebar lists **Production** and every environment. Picking one moves the whole project view, from Databases to Sites, into that environment, and a purple banner shows where the changes will land.

# Create an environment

{% only_dark %}
![Create environment page with the name, environment ID, and Start from options](/images/blog/announcing-project-environments/dark/create.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Create environment page with the name, environment ID, and Start from options](/images/blog/announcing-project-environments/create.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Open the environment switcher and click **Create environment**. Give it a name, such as `Staging`, and an environment ID. The ID follows the name, so `Staging` becomes `staging`, and it's what apps pass to reach the environment.

Under **Start from**, choose an **Empty environment** for a fresh backend, or a **Clone of the project** to start from production's shape.

# Copy only what you need

{% only_dark %}
![What to copy list with structure selected for every group and data unticked](/images/blog/announcing-project-environments/dark/copy.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![What to copy list with structure selected for every group and data unticked](/images/blog/announcing-project-environments/copy.avif)
{% /only_light %}

A clone copies the structure and settings of every part of the project by default: databases with their tables, columns, and indexes, buckets, teams and auth settings, functions and sites with their variables and active deployments, messaging providers and topics, platforms, SMTP, and email templates. Rows, files, and users stay behind unless you tick them, so a staging environment gets production's shape without production's customer data.

Resources keep their IDs in the copy, so code that reads the `orders` table works in both. API keys, webhooks, and custom domains are never copied, because they belong to production. The copy runs in the background, and the environment is usable while it finishes.

# Connect your app

{% only_dark %}
![Connect to this environment card with the endpoint, project ID, and environment ID](/images/blog/announcing-project-environments/dark/connect.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Connect to this environment card with the endpoint, project ID, and environment ID](/images/blog/announcing-project-environments/connect.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Every environment's **Get started** page lists the endpoint, project ID, and environment ID. SDKs take the environment ID next to the project ID:

```client-web
import { Client } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setEnvironment('staging');
```

Leave `setEnvironment` out and the same code talks to production. Reading the environment ID from configuration lets one build of a frontend serve preview deployments against `staging` and the release build against production. Server SDKs work the same way, with an API key created inside the environment, so a production key can never reach staging and a staging key can never reach production.

# Promote changes from dev to staging to production

The Appwrite CLI reads the environment from `appwrite.config.json` or from the `APPWRITE_ENVIRONMENT_ID` variable. That makes a release pipeline a matter of running the same deploy with a different environment ID and key:

```sh
export APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID="<PROJECT_ID>"
export APPWRITE_ENVIRONMENT_ID=staging
appwrite push all --all --force
```

A CI job can push every merge to `staging`, then push the same config to production on release by leaving the environment ID empty. The [development workflow guide](/docs/advanced/environments/workflow) has a complete GitHub Actions pipeline for it.

# What environments unlock

- **Safer schema changes.** Add a column, change an index, or restructure a table in `dev`, and push it to production only once the app works against it.
- **Real QA.** Reviewers get a backend with production's structure, seeded test accounts, and their own data to break.
- **A sandbox for AI agents.** The [Appwrite MCP server](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers#environments) takes an `environment_id`, so an agent can build features against a development environment with a full backend to change and production's data out of its path.
- **Preview deployments with a backend of their own.** Frontend previews can point at `staging` instead of production.

# Plans and limits

Environments are available on the Free, Pro, and Enterprise plans of Appwrite Cloud. Free and Pro projects get five environments each, and Enterprise projects get 25 by default. They don't use a project slot and aren't charged as additional projects; their usage simply adds to the project's.

Environments are built for development and testing, so each one holds up to 100 users and serves 500 API requests per hour, with Console requests not counted. Production traffic stays on the project, where it always was.

# Build with confidence

Every team deserves a place to try things that isn't production. Environments give each Appwrite project that place without a second project to keep in sync, so changes can be built, reviewed, and tested on a real backend before your users ever see them. We can't wait to see how they change the way your team ships. Try them on your next feature, and tell us how it goes on [Discord](https://appwrite.io/discord).

